page ref_ws_types {
  title = "wscript API: Term, types and the vmlab module"
  chapter_header "wscript API: Term, types and the vmlab module" {
    kicker       = "Reference · reference"
    reading_time = "10 min read"
    updated      = "2026-09-02"
    version      = "vmlab 0.9"
  }

  p <<DOC
    This chapter covers the rest of the wscript surface: `Term`, the
    send/expect handle [Machine.terminal](ref_ws_machine) returns; the six
    record types methods return or handlers receive; and the two functions
    on the `vmlab` module itself. The [scripting guide](guide_scripting)
    explains where scripts run. The handles are in
    [wscript API: Lab and Segment](ref_ws_lab) and
    [wscript API: Machine](ref_ws_machine).
  DOC

  p <<DOC
    The record types are plain structs: a script reads their fields
    directly, as `r.exit_code` or `login.password`. They are never
    constructed by a script. The signatures use the parameter names the host
    registers; the generated interface file spells them `a0`, `a1` and so
    on.
  DOC

  h2 "Term.send"
  p "Send raw text to the shell."
  code wscript { source = "fn send(self, text: string) -> Result[unit, string]" }
  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`text`" | "string" | "The bytes to write to the PTY, exactly as given. No newline is added." |
  }
  p <<DOC
    Use this for control characters and partial input; the example below
    sends Ctrl-C as the byte 0x03. It fails with "<machine>: terminal session is closed" after
    `Term.close` or after the shell has exited, and with the agent's error if
    the write fails.
  DOC
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
fn main(lab: Lab) {
    let Ok(vm) = lab.vm("box") else { return }
    let Ok(t) = vm.terminal() else { return }
    let s = t.send("\u{3}")
    t.close()
}
SRC
  }

  h2 "Term.send_line"
  p "Send a line of input followed by Enter."
  code wscript { source = "fn send_line(self, text: string) -> Result[unit, string]" }
  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`text`" | "string" | "The line to type." |
  }
  p <<DOC
    Writes `text` and a carriage return, which is what a PTY expects from
    Enter and works for POSIX shells and PowerShell alike. The errors are
    those of `Term.send`.
  DOC
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
fn main(lab: Lab) {
    let Ok(vm) = lab.vm("box") else { return }
    let Ok(t) = vm.terminal() else { return }
    let s = t.send_line("systemctl restart app")
    t.close()
}
SRC
  }

  h2 "Term.read"
  p "Take whatever output the shell has produced so far."
  code wscript { source = "fn read(self) -> string" }
  p <<DOC
    Drains output already queued, waiting at most about 50 milliseconds for
    more, and returns it, clearing the buffer. It does not wait for a
    prompt; use `Term.expect` for that. It never fails: a closed session
    returns what was left in the buffer, or an empty string.
  DOC
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
fn main(lab: Lab) {
    let Ok(vm) = lab.vm("box") else { return }
    let Ok(t) = vm.terminal() else { return }
    vmlab::sleep_ms(500)
    lab.log(t.read())
    t.close()
}
SRC
  }

  h2 "Term.expect"
  p "Wait until the output matches a regular expression and return the text up to the end of the match."
  code wscript { source = "fn expect(self, pattern: string, timeout_secs: int) -> Result[string, string]" }
  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`pattern`" | "string" | "A regular expression, matched anywhere in the accumulated output." |
      | "`timeout_secs`" | "int" | "How long to wait, in seconds." |
  }
  p <<DOC
    Output accumulates in a buffer. On a match the text through the end of
    the match is consumed and returned, and what follows stays for the next
    call, so successive expects walk the stream. The buffer holds the raw PTY
    stream, prompts and escape sequences included, so match on something
    stable. It fails with "bad pattern: <reason>" for an invalid expression,
    with "<machine>: timed out after <n>s waiting for /<pattern>/; tail:
    <text>" at the deadline, and with "<machine>: terminal ended (<why>)
    before /<pattern>/ matched; tail: <text>" when the shell exits or the
    agent channel closes first. The tail is the last few hundred bytes of
    unmatched output, for debugging.
  DOC
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
fn main(lab: Lab) {
    let Ok(vm) = lab.vm("box") else { return }
    let Ok(t) = vm.terminal() else { return }
    let s = t.send_line("hostname")
    match t.expect("box", 10) {
        Ok(out) => lab.log("saw: " + out),
        Err(e) => lab.log(e),
    }
    t.close()
}
SRC
  }

  h2 "Term.resize"
  p "Resize the session's PTY."
  code wscript { source = "fn resize(self, cols: int, rows: int) -> Result[unit, string]" }
  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`cols`" | "int" | "The new width in columns. Clamped to at least 2." |
      | "`rows`" | "int" | "The new height in rows. Clamped to at least 2." |
  }
  p <<DOC
    A session opens at 120 by 32, which is wide enough that prompts rarely
    wrap mid-pattern. It fails on a closed session and with the agent's
    error otherwise.
  DOC
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
fn main(lab: Lab) {
    let Ok(vm) = lab.vm("box") else { return }
    let Ok(t) = vm.terminal() else { return }
    let rz = t.resize(200, 50)
    t.close()
}
SRC
  }

  h2 "Term.close"
  p "End the session and kill the shell."
  code wscript { source = "fn close(self)" }
  p <<DOC
    Closing is deterministic; a handle that goes out of scope without it is
    closed when the script's VM collects it. Closing twice is harmless. It
    never fails. After it, `Term.send`, `Term.send_line` and `Term.resize`
    fail with "terminal session is closed" and `Term.expect` reports the
    session ended with reason "closed".
  DOC
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
fn main(lab: Lab) {
    let Ok(vm) = lab.vm("box") else { return }
    let Ok(t) = vm.terminal() else { return }
    t.close()
}
SRC
  }

  h2 "Match"
  p "Where an image or text was found on the display, returned by the screen-matching methods."
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
struct Match {
    x: int,
    y: int,
    w: int,
    h: int,
    score: float,
    cx: int,
    cy: int,
    text: string,
}
SRC
  }
  table {
    rows:
      | "Field" | "Type" | "Default" | "Meaning" |
      | "`x`" | "int" | "0" | "Left edge of the match, in framebuffer pixels." |
      | "`y`" | "int" | "0" | "Top edge of the match." |
      | "`w`" | "int" | "0" | "Width of the matched region; the reference image's width." |
      | "`h`" | "int" | "0" | "Height of the matched region." |
      | "`score`" | "float" | "1.0" | "The similarity score, 0 to 1." |
      | "`cx`" | "int" | "0" | "Horizontal centre, for `Machine.mouse_move`." |
      | "`cy`" | "int" | "0" | "Vertical centre." |
      | "`text`" | "string" | "empty" | "The matched text, set only by `Machine.wait_for_text`." |
  }
  p <<DOC
    From `wait_for_image`, `wait_for_image_opts`, `wait_for_any` and
    `find_image` the position fields describe the match and `text` is empty.
    From `wait_for_text` only `text` is meaningful: the position fields are
    0 and the score is 1, because OCR does not report a location. The
    defaults column shows those filler values.
  DOC

  h2 "ExecResult"
  p "What a guest command produced, returned by `Machine.exec` and `Machine.exec_timeout`."
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
struct ExecResult {
    exit_code: int,
    stdout: string,
    stderr: string,
}
SRC
  }
  table {
    rows:
      | "Field" | "Type" | "Default" | "Meaning" |
      | "`exit_code`" | "int" | "none" | "The process's exit code. Non-zero is not an error to the call." |
      | "`stdout`" | "string" | "none" | "Captured standard output, decoded as UTF-8 with invalid bytes replaced." |
      | "`stderr`" | "string" | "none" | "Captured standard error, decoded the same way." |
  }
  p <<DOC
    Every field is always set. Branch on `exit_code` for the command's own
    verdict; the call's `Result` reports only whether the command could be
    run at all.
  DOC

  h2 "Login"
  p "One identity the lab file declares for a machine, returned by `Machine.logins`."
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
struct Login {
    label: string,
    user: string,
    password: Option[string],
    elevated: bool,
    default: bool,
}
SRC
  }
  table {
    rows:
      | "Field" | "Type" | "Default" | "Meaning" |
      | "`label`" | "string" | "none" | "The name an SSH username or `--user` selects this identity by." |
      | "`user`" | "string" | "none" | "The guest account, for example `PROBE\\dev`." |
      | "`password`" | "Option[string]" | "`None`" | "The declared secret exactly as written, or `None` where the author declared none. Never an empty string." |
      | "`elevated`" | "bool" | "true on Windows, false on Linux" | "Whether sessions as this login run elevated. Resolved: an undeclared value takes the guest family's default. Elevation is Windows-only, so a Linux login that never declared it reports false." |
      | "`default`" | "bool" | "resolved" | "Whether this is the machine's default identity, with the lone-login rule applied: a machine's only login is the default without saying so." |
  }
  p <<DOC
    `password` is `None` rather than empty so that a script never passes an
    empty secret to an account-creation command by accident. `elevated` and
    `default` cross resolved rather than as written, so a script asking "is
    this the default" gets the answer vmlab acts on. See
    [Logins and the SSH facade](guide_logins) and the `login {}` block in
    [vm and its children](ref_vm).
  DOC

  h2 "GuestStats"
  p "One sample of guest metrics, returned by `Machine.stats`."
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
struct GuestStats {
    cpu_pct: float,
    mem_used: int,
    mem_total: int,
    disks: List[DiskStat],
}
SRC
  }
  table {
    rows:
      | "Field" | "Type" | "Default" | "Meaning" |
      | "`cpu_pct`" | "float" | "none" | "CPU use across the guest, in percent." |
      | "`mem_used`" | "int" | "none" | "Memory in use, in bytes." |
      | "`mem_total`" | "int" | "none" | "Memory the guest sees, in bytes." |
      | "`disks`" | "List[DiskStat]" | "empty" | "One entry per mounted filesystem." |
  }
  p <<DOC
    The sample comes from the agent's metrics feature; `vmlab machine
    capabilities` says whether a machine's agent offers it.
  DOC

  h2 "DiskStat"
  p "One mounted filesystem inside `GuestStats`."
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
struct DiskStat {
    mount: string,
    used: int,
    total: int,
}
SRC
  }
  table {
    rows:
      | "Field" | "Type" | "Default" | "Meaning" |
      | "`mount`" | "string" | "none" | "The mount point, such as `/` or `C:\\`." |
      | "`used`" | "int" | "none" | "Bytes in use." |
      | "`total`" | "int" | "none" | "The filesystem's size in bytes." |
  }

  h2 "Event"
  p "The payload an event handler receives as its first argument."
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
struct Event {
    name: string,
    vm: string,
    data: string,
}

fn handle(event: Event, lab: Lab) { }
SRC
  }
  table {
    rows:
      | "Field" | "Type" | "Default" | "Meaning" |
      | "`name`" | "string" | "none" | "The event name, such as `vm.crashed`. See [Events](ref_events)." |
      | "`vm`" | "string" | "empty" | "The machine the event concerns: the payload's `vm` key, else its `container` key, else empty." |
      | "`data`" | "string" | "`{}`" | "The whole payload as JSON text." |
  }
  p <<DOC
    A handler is bound with an `on {}` block in the lab file, see
    [Events and handlers](guide_events), and runs `fn handle(event: Event,
    lab: Lab)` on a lab handle with no owning machine. Handler failures are
    logged and never fatal. `vm` is filled from the `vm` or `container` key
    only, so an event that names its subject as `machine` alone leaves it
    empty; the daemons set both keys on such events for that reason. `data`
    carries every other field for a handler to parse.
  DOC
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
fn handle(event: Event, lab: Lab) {
    lab.log("event " + event.name + " on " + event.vm)
    if event.name == "vm.crashed" {
        let Ok(m) = lab.machine(event.vm) else { return }
        let shot = m.screenshot("")
    }
}
SRC
  }

  h2 "vmlab::sleep_ms"
  p "Pause the script."
  code wscript { source = "fn sleep_ms(ms: int)" }
  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`ms`" | "int" | "How long to sleep, in milliseconds. A negative value returns at once." |
  }
  p <<DOC
    Blocks the script's thread; the lab daemon keeps running. Prefer the
    waiting methods, `Machine.wait_ready`, `Machine.wait_for_image`,
    `Term.expect`, where one fits, and use this for polling loops around
    `Machine.exec`.
  DOC
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
fn main(lab: Lab) {
    let vm = lab.this_vm().expect("no target vm")
    for i in 0..10 {
        match vm.exec("cmd.exe", ["/c", "if exist C:\\done (exit 0) else (exit 1)"]) {
            Ok(r) => { if r.exit_code == 0 { return } }
            Err(e) => lab.log("waiting: " + e),
        }
        vmlab::sleep_ms(1000)
    }
}
SRC
  }

  h2 "vmlab::env"
  p "Read an environment variable of the lab daemon's process."
  code wscript { source = "fn env(name: string) -> string" }
  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`name`" | "string" | "The variable's name." |
  }
  p <<DOC
    Returns the value, or an empty string when it is unset, so a script
    cannot tell an unset variable from an empty one. The environment is the
    daemon's, which inherits from the `vmlab` invocation that started it,
    not necessarily from the shell running `vmlab up` now. It exists so a
    build or provision script can carry an operator toggle without a schema
    change, such as a `VMLAB_SKIP_UPDATES=1` that lets a template build
    skip its update pass.
  DOC
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
fn main(lab: Lab) {
    if vmlab::env("VMLAB_SKIP_UPDATES") == "1" {
        lab.log("skipping updates")
        return
    }
}
SRC
  }
}
